Description: xcb: do not delete input devices twice on hierarchy changes
 Slave devices are QObject children of their master, so deleting a removed
 master deletes its slaves with it. A hierarchy change that removes a master
 must therefore keep its slaves out of the qDeleteAll list.
 .
 This is reachable because slaves that X stops attaching are reported as
 floating slaves, a type the device query loop below skips, so they stay in
 the list of devices to delete. Deleting the master frees such a slave
 through QObjectPrivate::deleteChildren(), and qDeleteAll then deletes the
 same pointer again, corrupting the heap. The crash happens later, often in
 an unrelated allocation; under ASAN it is a heap-use-after-free in
 qDeleteAll.
 .
 Filter out entries whose parent is also being removed before deleting.
 The loop runs backwards so that removeAt() keeps the remaining indices
 valid; only slaves are ever dropped, because a master's parent is the
 QXcbConnection object.
Author: JiDe Zhang <zhangjide@uniontech.com>
Origin: backport, https://codereview.qt-project.org/c/qt/qtbase/+/791760
Bug: https://bugreports.qt.io/browse/QTBUG-152681
Applied-Upstream: commit:00bdf5fabe80dcd0a5fe5679e1d166cdc31a9199
Last-Update: 2026-11-26
---
diff --git a/src/plugins/platforms/xcb/qxcbconnection_xi2.cpp b/src/plugins/platforms/xcb/qxcbconnection_xi2.cpp
index 17a24cdbc9c..749e40ed665 100644
--- a/src/plugins/platforms/xcb/qxcbconnection_xi2.cpp
+++ b/src/plugins/platforms/xcb/qxcbconnection_xi2.cpp
@@ -544,6 +544,18 @@ void QXcbConnection::xi2SetupDevices()
         m_xiSlavePointerIds.removeAll(id);
         m_touchDevices.remove(id);
     }
+    // A master is the QObject parent of its slaves, so deleting it deletes them
+    // too. Slaves that X no longer attaches stay in previousDevices (the query
+    // loops above skip XCB_INPUT_DEVICE_TYPE_FLOATING_SLAVE), so deleting the
+    // master would destroy them and qDeleteAll would then delete the same
+    // pointers again, corrupting the heap. Drop the children of removed devices
+    // first; walk backwards so that removeAt() does not invalidate the
+    // iteration.
+    for (qsizetype i = previousDevices.size() - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
+        const auto *parentDevice = qobject_cast<const QInputDevice *>(previousDevices.at(i)->parent());
+        if (parentDevice && previousDevices.contains(parentDevice))
+            previousDevices.removeAt(i);
+    }
     qDeleteAll(previousDevices);
 
     if (m_xiMasterPointerIds.size() > 1)
